# Mock Model Calls with ReqLLM

## Overview

Every Jido AI model call goes through ReqLLM, so ReqLLM is where your tests
replace the provider. You can mock it at two depths: the HTTP request ReqLLM
sends, or the `ReqLLM.generate_text/3` call itself. Both run offline with no
provider key.

This Livebook runs the same Agents through both paths. The HTTP mock
asserts the exact provider request body: roles, tool definitions, and the
JSON schema for structured output. The Mimic mock asserts the exact model,
`ReqLLM.Context`, and options that Jido AI hands to ReqLLM.

## Install

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido_ai, "~> 3.0.0-beta.1"},
  {:plug, "~> 1.0"},
  {:mimic, "~> 2.0"}
])
```

```elixir
Mimic.copy(ReqLLM)
ExUnit.start(autorun: false)
```

`Req.Test` ships with Req and needs Plug. Mimic is only for the second path.

## Choose a Path

| | HTTP mock with `Req.Test` | Mimic on `ReqLLM` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Replaces | The provider HTTP endpoint | `ReqLLM.generate_text/3` and friends |
| You assert | The JSON body ReqLLM sends | The model, `ReqLLM.Context`, and options |
| You return | Provider JSON, such as OpenAI Responses API | A `ReqLLM.Response` struct |
| Covers | Jido AI, ReqLLM encoding, and response decoding | Jido AI only |
| Request setup | `options.req_http_options: [plug: ...]` per request | None; the Agent runs unchanged |
| Async tests | Yes | No; `set_mimic_global` needs `async: false` |
| Provider-specific | Yes; the body and responses follow one provider | No |

Use the HTTP mock when the provider wire format matters: tool definitions,
`json_schema` output, or a ReqLLM upgrade. Use Mimic for everything else. It
asserts the boundary Jido AI owns, and the fake responses stay small.

## The Agents

A tool-using support Agent and a triage Agent with no tools:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.LookupOrder do
  use Jido.Action,
    id: "my_app/actions/lookup_order",
    vsn: 1,
    name: "lookup_order",
    description: "Look up one order by ID.",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.object(%{order_id: Zoi.string(), status: Zoi.string(), eta: Zoi.string()})

  @impl Jido.Action
  def run(%{order_id: order_id}, _context) do
    {:ok, %{order_id: order_id, status: "shipped", eta: "2026-11-02"}}
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.SupportAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "support_agent",
    id: "my_app/support_agent",
    vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
      instructions "Answer customer support questions. Use tools for order data."
    end

    tools do
      action MyApp.LookupOrder
    end
  end
end

defmodule MyApp.TriageAgent do
  use Jido.AI.Agent,
    name: "triage_agent",
    id: "my_app/triage_agent",
    vsn: 1

  ai do
    reasoning Jido.AI.Reasoning.ReAct do
      model "openai:gpt-4o-mini"
      instructions "Classify the support ticket."
    end
  end
end
```

## HTTP Mock Responses

`openai:` models use the OpenAI Responses API. The offline model is a small
module that builds its JSON. `offline/1` adds the request options that route
ReqLLM to the `Req.Test` plug named `MyApp.LLM`:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.OpenAIResponses do
  def offline(params) do
    Map.put(params, :options, %{
      api_key: "offline-test-key",
      max_retries: 0,
      max_tokens: 128,
      temperature: 0.0,
      req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]
    })
  end

  def text(text, input_tokens \\ 20, output_tokens \\ 6) do
    response(
      [
        %{
          "type" => "message",
          "id" => "msg-1",
          "role" => "assistant",
          "status" => "completed",
          "content" => [
            %{"type" => "output_text", "text" => text, "annotations" => [], "logprobs" => []}
          ]
        }
      ],
      input_tokens,
      output_tokens
    )
  end

  def tool_call(call_id, name, arguments) do
    response(
      [
        %{
          "type" => "function_call",
          "id" => "fc-1",
          "call_id" => call_id,
          "name" => name,
          "arguments" => Jason.encode!(arguments),
          "status" => "completed"
        }
      ],
      20,
      4
    )
  end

  def body(conn) do
    {:ok, body, _conn} = Plug.Conn.read_body(conn)
    Jason.decode!(body)
  end

  defp response(output, input_tokens, output_tokens) do
    %{
      "id" => "resp-1",
      "object" => "response",
      "created_at" => 1,
      "model" => "gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18",
      "status" => "completed",
      "error" => nil,
      "incomplete_details" => nil,
      "parallel_tool_calls" => true,
      "output" => output,
      "usage" => %{
        "input_tokens" => input_tokens,
        "input_tokens_details" => %{"cached_tokens" => 0},
        "output_tokens" => output_tokens,
        "output_tokens_details" => %{"reasoning_tokens" => 0},
        "total_tokens" => input_tokens + output_tokens
      }
    }
  end
end
```

`options` replaces the Flow's default options map. Set `max_tokens` and
`temperature` again, or ReqLLM applies its own defaults. `max_retries: 0`
stops ReqLLM from retrying a failed mock response.

## Assert the Body

Each `Req.Test.expect/2` answers one HTTP request, in order. The plug sends
the decoded body to the test process, so the test asserts on it after the
request finishes:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.HTTPMockTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  alias Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply
  alias MyApp.OpenAIResponses

  setup {Req.Test, :set_req_test_from_context}
  setup {Req.Test, :verify_on_exit!}

  @triage %{
    "type" => "object",
    "properties" => %{
      "category" => %{"type" => "string", "enum" => ["billing", "shipping", "account"]},
      "priority" => %{"type" => "string", "enum" => ["low", "normal", "high"]},
      "summary" => %{"type" => "string"}
    },
    "required" => ["category", "priority", "summary"],
    "additionalProperties" => false
  }

  test "a tool round sends roles, tools, and the tool result" do
    owner = self()

    Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
      send(owner, {:body, OpenAIResponses.body(conn)})

      Req.Test.json(
        conn,
        OpenAIResponses.tool_call("call-1", "lookup_order", %{order_id: "A-1001"})
      )
    end)

    Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
      send(owner, {:body, OpenAIResponses.body(conn)})
      Req.Test.json(conn, OpenAIResponses.text("Order A-1001 shipped.", 30, 6))
    end)

    agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-1")

    signal =
      MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(OpenAIResponses.offline(%{input: "Where is A-1001?"}))

    assert {:ok, _agent, [%Reply{signal: reply}]} = MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)
    assert reply.data.status == :completed
    assert reply.data.output == "Order A-1001 shipped."

    assert_receive {:body, first}
    assert_receive {:body, second}

    assert first["model"] == "gpt-4o-mini"
    assert first["max_output_tokens"] == 128
    assert Enum.map(first["input"], & &1["role"]) == ["system", "user"]
    assert [%{"name" => "lookup_order", "type" => "function", "strict" => true}] = first["tools"]
    assert first["tools"] |> hd() |> get_in(["parameters", "required"]) == ["order_id"]

    assert Enum.map(second["input"], &{&1["role"], &1["type"]}) == [
             {"system", nil},
             {"user", nil},
             {nil, "function_call"},
             {nil, "function_call_output"}
           ]
  end

  test "structured output sends a strict json_schema" do
    owner = self()
    triage = %{category: "shipping", priority: "normal", summary: "Customer asks about A-1001."}

    Req.Test.expect(MyApp.LLM, fn conn ->
      send(owner, {:body, OpenAIResponses.body(conn)})
      Req.Test.json(conn, OpenAIResponses.text(Jason.encode!(triage)))
    end)

    agent = MyApp.TriageAgent.instantiate!(id: "triage-1")

    signal =
      MyApp.TriageAgent.request_signal!(
        OpenAIResponses.offline(%{input: "Where is order A-1001?", output_schema: @triage})
      )

    assert {:ok, _agent, [%Reply{signal: reply}]} = MyApp.TriageAgent.cmd(agent, signal)

    assert reply.data.output == %{
             "category" => "shipping",
             "priority" => "normal",
             "summary" => "Customer asks about A-1001."
           }

    assert_receive {:body, body}
    refute Map.has_key?(body, "tools")

    assert %{
             "type" => "json_schema",
             "name" => "output_schema",
             "strict" => true,
             "schema" => schema
           } =
             body["text"]["format"]

    assert schema["required"] == ["category", "priority", "summary"]
  end
end
```

The second request carries the tool exchange as `function_call` and
`function_call_output` items. That is ReqLLM's OpenAI encoding of the
`:assistant` and `:tool` messages that Jido AI projected.

An Agent with tools encodes structured output differently: ReqLLM adds a
`structured_output` function tool and forces it with `tool_choice`. Assert
the encoding your Agent sends.

## Mock ReqLLM with Mimic

`expect(ReqLLM, :generate_text, fn model, context, options -> ... end)`
replaces one call. The function receives exactly what Jido AI passes to
ReqLLM. The request needs no extra options:

```elixir
defmodule MyApp.MimicMockTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  import Mimic

  alias Jido.Agent.Directive.Reply

  setup :set_mimic_global
  setup :verify_on_exit!

  test "Jido AI sends the exact model, Context, and options" do
    expect(ReqLLM, :generate_text, fn model, context, options ->
      assert model == "openai:gpt-4o-mini"

      assert [system, user] = ReqLLM.Context.to_list(context)
      assert system.role == :system

      assert hd(system.content).text ==
               "Answer customer support questions. Use tools for order data."

      assert user.role == :user
      assert hd(user.content).text == "Where is A-1001?"

      assert options |> Keyword.keys() |> Enum.sort() ==
               [:max_tokens, :receive_timeout, :telemetry, :temperature, :tools, :total_timeout]

      assert options[:max_tokens] == 128
      assert options[:temperature] == 0.0
      assert options[:receive_timeout] == 30_000
      assert [%ReqLLM.Tool{name: "lookup_order"}] = options[:tools]

      {:ok,
       %ReqLLM.Response{
         id: "resp-1",
         model: model,
         context: context,
         finish_reason: :stop,
         message: ReqLLM.Context.assistant("Order A-1001 shipped."),
         usage: %{input_tokens: 20, output_tokens: 6}
       }}
    end)

    agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-1")
    signal = MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{input: "Where is A-1001?"})

    assert {:ok, _agent, [%Reply{signal: reply}]} = MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)
    assert reply.data.output == "Order A-1001 shipped."
    assert reply.data.usage.total_tokens == 26
  end

  test "structured output arrives as a strict ReqLLM.Output" do
    expect(ReqLLM, :generate_text, fn model, context, options ->
      assert %ReqLLM.Output{type: :object} = options[:output]
      assert options[:output_validation] == :strict

      {:ok,
       %ReqLLM.Response{
         id: "resp-1",
         model: model,
         context: context,
         finish_reason: :stop,
         object: %{
           "category" => "shipping",
           "priority" => "normal",
           "summary" => "Where is A-1001?"
         },
         usage: %{input_tokens: 20, output_tokens: 6}
       }}
    end)

    agent = MyApp.TriageAgent.instantiate!(id: "triage-1")

    signal =
      MyApp.TriageAgent.request_signal!(%{
        input: "Where is A-1001?",
        output_schema: %{
          "type" => "object",
          "properties" => %{"category" => %{"type" => "string"}},
          "required" => ["category"]
        }
      })

    assert {:ok, _agent, [%Reply{signal: reply}]} = MyApp.TriageAgent.cmd(agent, signal)
    assert reply.data.output["category"] == "shipping"
  end

  test "stream: true calls ReqLLM.stream_text/3 instead" do
    expect(ReqLLM, :stream_text, fn model, context, _options ->
      response = %ReqLLM.Response{
        id: "resp-1",
        model: model,
        context: context,
        finish_reason: :stop,
        message: ReqLLM.Context.assistant("Order A-1001 shipped."),
        usage: %{input_tokens: 20, output_tokens: 6}
      }

      {:ok, ReqLLM.Cache.stream_response(response, ReqLLM.model!(model), context)}
    end)

    agent = MyApp.SupportAgent.instantiate!(id: "support-1")
    signal = MyApp.SupportAgent.request_signal!(%{input: "Where is A-1001?", stream: true})

    assert {:ok, _agent, [%Reply{signal: reply}]} = MyApp.SupportAgent.cmd(agent, signal)
    assert reply.data.output == "Order A-1001 shipped."
  end
end
```

`total_timeout` is the time left in the request's `limits.elapsed_ms` budget,
so its value changes on every run. Assert that the key is present, not its
value. `telemetry` carries the Thread ID as `conversation_id`.

`ReqLLM.Cache.stream_response/3` turns a complete `ReqLLM.Response` into a
`ReqLLM.StreamResponse`, so one builder serves both modes.
[Stream Model Output](stream-model-output.livemd) covers the stream Signals.

## Run the Tests

```elixir
ExUnit.run()
```

The result is `%{total: 5, failures: 0, excluded: 0, skipped: 0}`.

## Summary

- The HTTP mock routes ReqLLM to `Req.Test` with
  `options.req_http_options: [plug: {Req.Test, MyApp.LLM}]` and asserts the
  provider JSON body.
- An Agent without tools sends structured output as a strict `json_schema`.
  An Agent with tools sends a `structured_output` function tool.
- Mimic replaces `ReqLLM.generate_text/3` and asserts the exact model,
  `ReqLLM.Context`, and options, with no request changes.
- `stream: true` calls `ReqLLM.stream_text/3`; mock that function instead.
- Use `async: true` with the HTTP mock and `async: false` with Mimic global
  mode.

## Next

[Public API Reference](public-api-reference.md) lists every request field,
response field, and Signal name you can assert on.
